 officers and firefighters as  well actually  are prohibited from striking and so when  they sit down to bargain  with their employer which is the city  they  both sides know that the the strike is  not on the table  however there's other ways that cops can  kind of  withhold their labor and be  non-cooperative  to get the kind of contracts they would  like from the city  and the city unlike an you know let's  say like a private  you know steel mill operator or uh  you know uh i don't know any other type  of private business owner  or manager there's there's like limited  there's limited ways that uh that that  kind of withholding of the labor by the  police officers  can be mitigated so you can't you know  you can't easily bring in scabs  essentially to  to fill in the gap that's left by  striking workers or workers they're  withholding their labor in some way  so really what we see time and again um  and i'm specifically especially in  chicago  is the municipality is sort of like  they're really at the mercy of the union  in this in the sense and  historically this is why municipalities  were so uh  resistant to having police unions  because the kind of  view of local officials and also  of of federal judges that litigated you  know decided over lawsuits over  uh police unionization throughout the  20th century was that  police officers really aren't workers  like other types of workers  they are a paramedic military  organization  and just like the military they have to  have their one allegiance be  to their employer to the state that they  serve and they shouldn't have any other  type of  uh you know interest involved in this  and so now that police officers uh are  able to unionize  it's it's really a dynamic in which  they're able to  get a lot more in their contracts and  get a lot more concessions and a lot  more  bargain from a much stronger position  than any other really public or private  sector  uh workforce is this where the uh  unions were able to sort of gain enough  leverage  to develop some of the defaults that are  in place now  in terms of immunity or is that actually  coming from some other structure  yeah so there's as far as the um again  and i know specifics about the the  police contracts in chicago so really  i'm speaking from  from from this place yeah but the a lot  of the  the first the very first police union  contract that police officers in chicago  had which came  in 1980 really started at  with its bedrock being the the  policeman's bill of rights which  uh was modeled on the kind of first  amendment  uh bill of rights type of protections  that developed for criminal suspects and  and people accused of crimes  in the u.s constitution in the in the  20th century and so as that was  happening in the mid-century police  officers who were increasingly accused  of misconduct that were increasingly  dealing with harsher and hearts or disa  discipline from municipalities  they developed this sort of view that  the police need a bill of rights and  they need protections when they're  accused of some kind of brutality or  misconduct  and so the bedrock of the contract here  in chicago has always been this bill of  rights that protects officers from  having to make statements about  uh their behavior on the job in a  certain constraint of time  they're able to know who is accusing  them of their crimes they're able to  coordinate with one another before they  give statements they're able to amend  their statements after  video or audio evidence about the  incident becomes available  so really the core of the contract is  about protecting officers who are  accused of some kind of misconduct  and beyond that there's a lot of other  things like uh provisions that would  require destruction of misconduct  records  there's all kinds of stuff obviously  that's more on the labor side of things  about  hours and overtime and vacation and all  of that uh but basically  yeah the the contract is is now  extremely elaborate  and uh it really it's really  much more um there's much more that off  every time there's something new that's  in the contract which also  of course depends on the political  situation in which the contract is  decided  every time something new is added it's  much more difficult to take it away  and to to renegotiate the contract so  what we see time after time  is uh especially throughout the 90s the  contract becomes more elaborate there's  there's more and more protections and  guarantees for officers  and less and less space for the city to  come in and really radically change  things  in order to say change how officers are  disciplined for example  so in this context when the defund the  police  movement i guess if we call it that uh  got some momentum a few months ago  one of the things that that i said was  i'm unsure that defund is the right  approach  in in all cases and that there are  but my this is just to give you context  for the upcoming discussion  my thought was there there are cases  where there's too much funding for  certain things like militarization of  police there's other cases where  actually  being underfunded leads to some of the  sort of lower hanging fruit policing  that actually causes the problems we're  trying to solve anyway my point was  i laid out some ideas that i think would  be very useful in actually getting at  some of the problems in policing which  include for example a national bad  actors database  to take away the possibility of a police  officer  leaving one department for uh bad  actions being hired elsewhere in the new  department saying we had no idea there  was no way we could have known that  that's what that that's what took place  that this  individual had a bad uh act in their  past  now the obvious obstacle to that would  be every police union would oppose such  a database right i mean is it is it  is it even conceivable that something  like that could get past unions  yeah so every every police union would  oppose that and also  in a lot of places the police the  contracts do  actually provide for the destruction of  police misconduct records  after a certain amount of years wow so  even if you  were wanted to create such a database in  a lot of places you just couldn't  because the records wouldn't even exist  anymore i mean illinois is really an  exceptional case because we've had a lot  of civil rights litigation on this issue  and basically this past june actually  the illinois supreme court decided on a  case  that was um launched that was started by  a  independent journalist and civil rights  activist jamie calvin  in calvin versus chicago the illinois  supreme court decided that  the destruction of police misconduct  records  which were it turned out that the city  wasn't doing it even though the police  union contract said that they were  supposed  to be destroying them after five years  turned out they weren't doing it we've  got records going back to the 60s  and the illinois supreme court found  that uh  actually it's a dead letter provision in  the police union contract that these  records can't and shouldn't be destroyed  because it's a matter of public interest  to continue having them so technically  like in the state of illinois  you could have uh some kind of statewide  database where like  if a cop gets fired which by the way is  extremely extremely rare  for a cop to even have any kind of  discipline based on misconduct  allegations or complaints  but in the rare occasions where they are  disciplined or fired  you know you could technically have this  kind of database that would prevent them  from them going across to a different  municipality and and getting a job there  but i think that one of the other things  and kind of brought this up with your  earlier question about  about the immunity that that officers  have  that the kind of uh immunity from  prosecution for their conduct on the job  what's sometimes called qualified  immunity  or indemnification that stuff doesn't  really  stem from the union contracts themselves  those are provisions that are installed  by state law  so it's the state that indemnifies  officers against  uh essentially liability for the things  they do on the job which is then  why whenever a cop uh you know does  something bad  they are it's the city that has to be  responsible for that conduct it's the  city that gets sued it's the city that  has to pay  and now the conversation that i'm  hearing more and more is about how we  should actually require officers  to carry liability insurance of some  sort for misconduct like sort of like  malpractice insurance for doctors  uh so that they are themselves  financially liable  for their behavior and then if they  mess up enough times then no nobody's  gonna wanna insure them and that's how  you get these kind of  quote-unquote bad apples out of the  force well  that conversation is really dead in the  water as long as there are these state  laws indemnifying officers and on doing  them would be like  a massive massive battle especially in a  state like illinois where  you know it's very blue up in chicago  but then the rest of state is pretty red  uh it would be hard to get republican  state legislation on board for  changing the syndic indemnification  thing but  yeah that's just an additional layer of  complication aside from the contract  that already provides so many  protections for officers  yeah and you know i i think it's  important to be balanced in this  conversation from from the standpoint of  uh as someone like myself who is on the  left and i am concerned  about the state of policing in the  united states i also do recognize that  being a police officer  is different than working in a factory  in a lot of different ways there is a  public responsibility that comes with it  there  is an element of although it's  relatively rare for an officer to be  killed in the line of duty statistically  speaking  it is a possibility that is that is more  real there than in a number of other  jobs so i think the quest  the concern i have is have the has the  infrastructure as you're pointing out  fallen out of balance in the sense that  a lot of this stuff  that leads to problems is codified in  state law as you're pointing out  there's disproportionate power dynamics  and so i guess what i'm trying to point  out to my audience is  i recognize that being a police officer  is different than a lot of other jobs  and in some senses it should be treated  differently but a lot of the  infrastructure you're pointing out  seems to have fallen a little bit out of  balance  yeah there's that and there's also a  third really important  uh piece of this which is uh the culture  inside police departments and that is  the piece that it's  that it's most difficult to kind of  tinker with  or create interventions to change  because  the contract is an expression of a  certain kind of culture in the  department  the uh behavior of officers that we see  time and time and time again  is it is not just an expression of  training it's not just an expression of  the expectations or the rigor or lack  thereof  of the disciplinary apparatus inside the  department it's also an expression of  the culture  and when you have a culture at a police  department that for example  like in chicago you have a situation  where officers get out of the academy  they get connected with their first  field trading officers  and routinely the field training  officers tell the new recruits  forget everything you learn in the  academy this is how we do this job  and then they teach them illegal kind of  techniques that have been found time and  again by the justice department  to be a violation of civil rights it's  it's there's a kind of mentality in a  culture that gets taught  oftentimes it's reinforced already  because  a lot of folks that decide to join the  police department  grow up in neighborhoods where there's a  lot of police neighbors they may be from  police families  it's a kind of intergenerational thing  so personally i think if  we're talking about changing the way  that policing operates in this country  it's not just a matter of  tinkering with the contract it's not  just a matter of changing the laws in  terms of what officers are allowed to do  it's also about addressing culture how  are you going to address the  culture in which members of the  department are walking around with  punisher skulls  on their uniforms you know how are you  going to address a culture in which  members of the department  are making like racist statements in  online you know in online forums  completely openly so that kind of stuff  i think will be  probably difficult to change without  like serious changes in the way that  like these jobs are paid  and uh how promotion works and how you  get ahead in a job like this  and frankly the more the more i see of  what's going on  um this summer the more i think that  like the kind of culture around  weapons and and the fact that this is a  job that  requires you to carry a gun there's an  increasingly as you point out  militarized component to it  um that stuff i think that if if we  don't address how police officers are  armed and what kind of weaponry  uh young men who might want to join the  force the young women who might join the  force  have access to as part of this job we're  probably not going to see a change in  culture because we're going to keep  attracting the same type of people to  this job  well it's it seems to be a very similar  problem to the gun culture problem in  the united states where when we talk  about well look in switzerland  it's this easy to get a firearm or you  can find this other area where everybody  has guns and there's no problem  part of it may be laws but a lot of it  also  is the relationship of americans to  firearms and  and unless you change that you're going  to have a tough tough time but this  actually 