relates to the policing  question which is  when there are 350 million firearms out  in the united states and  it is true that anybody police come  across  could have a firearm on them and and  possibly  aren't particularly well well trained  with it or whatever the case may be  because it's so easy to get firearms  it seems difficult to deal with the  issue of police and firearms without  dealing with the issue of  350 million firearms in society right  exactly and i think that  as long probably as long as we have such  a heavily armed  general population and people feel like  their only way of being protected and  it's actually true that for some people  their only way of having some kind of  sense of protection and security  especially people living in some of the  poorest neighborhoods in this country  is having a weapon because there  because there's inter-communal  interpersonal violence that happens  regularly that the police do nothing  about  they do nothing to stop um you know  yeah you're gonna have any kind of  movement to disarm the police would  probably  uh haven't even be even more difficult  to  to accomplish its goals than that than  the kind of  defund the police movement that we're  seeing now but  in general like i feel like this whole  situation in kenosha right now  the with this young man who uh  the 17 year old who shot people killed  killed people  you know his whole social media presence  was about supporting the police  supporting president trump you know  being part of a militia taking the  situation in their own hands like  something there's some there's a bleed  over  around a culture of violence and a  culture that like glorifies weapons  and and and and police culture in this  country so  we can at one on the one hand try to  tinker around the edges  of of the of police contracts and the  sort of bureaucracy and  infrastructure that exists to perpetuate  the system  and on the other hand like not address  the culture that's  already existing in these spaces and  allows them to to kind of thrive and  persist  so it's it's a complicated thing to  untangle and  definitely uh the the thing that's in  the power  of our local elected officials and uh  municipalities  is is to not cede so much ground  in the police contract negotiations all  uh complexly intertwined issues as you  say  untangling is probably the right phrase  to uh to apply to it  we've been speaking with maya dukemaseva  who's a senior writer at the chicago  reader  amaya really appreciate your time today
 

